ke  that added to the supply of government  debt but the third factor in particular  was the mini budget so one has to say of  all the events it was the mini budget  that was the most significant but the  backdrop was both those global factors  in the Bank of England last Thursday and  in terms of the mini budget there were  lots of good things in the many budget  in terms of aims at the pro-growth  agenda Confederation the British  industry like to The Institute of  directors liked it that as I warned  about beforehand and as I was worried  out on Dave and as the markets are now  showing the bank at the markets were not  liking the fact that the government  borrowed too much they announced the  whole series of measures that the market  expected but then they went that one  extra step they announced some tax  changes that the markets were not  comfortable with I think the markets  were overly worried about the  inflationary implications of those extra  tax changes but in particular and I can  understand that the markets were worried  about the affordability largely because  they were not prepared for it so for  instance the tax change for higher  earners in economic terms was not so  significant but it was like the last  straw that broke the camels back the  chancellor while he had focused on the  general public and on British businesses  he had not really prepared the financial  markets fully and I think he had taken  his eye off the ball slightly shall we  say in having not prepared the markets  for what he was doing in the budget and  I felt that he overstepped the mark last  week so it was the combination of all  three factors the febrile markets  because the global backdrop the actions  of the bank of England last Thursday but  let's be no doubt it was primarily the  mini budget last Friday that triggered  this later series of events  I have to say listening to you I am  quite struck by the change in the  attitude that I am seeing when we spoke  before the budg